Born in Ascalon, Judea, Antiochus studied under Philo of Larissa, the head of the New Academy, but eventually diverged sharply from his teacher's skeptical stance. Around 86 BCE, he founded his own school in Athens, known as the 'Old Academy,' signaling his commitment to restoring what he believed was the true Platonic tradition. His philosophical output, though largely lost to time, is primarily known through the extensive writings of Cicero, who attended Antiochus's lectures in Athens in 79–78 BCE and engaged critically with his ideas in works like *Academica* and *De Finibus*. Antiochus argued for the possibility of certain knowledge and the essential harmony between the major Hellenistic schools on ethical and metaphysical questions, emphasizing shared principles such as the supremacy of virtue and the existence of an ordered cosmos."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:47:05Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33518442328", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "123 BCE, Ashkelon"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "123 BCE, Ashkelon"}], "newValue": ["The prevailing Academic Skepticism of the New Academy created a philosophical vacuum for those seeking certain knowledge.", "The intellectual climate of the late Hellenistic period fostered a desire to reconcile and synthesize diverse philosophical traditions.", "The historical memory of Plato's original teachings provided a powerful impetus for a return to perceived foundational truths."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-01T14:47:05Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#33518442328", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "67 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks 